course: dictionary
lesson: 7262
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and pike is one of them. The context, the words and sentences around it, tells you which meaning is right. «The pike waited under the bridge for smaller fish.» Waiting for fish and living in water tell you this pike is a fish.
explain: «The soldiers lowered their pikes and marched towards the castle.» Soldiers marching at a castle, with something held in the hands, points to a long weapon. Ask yourself which meaning fits, then try it in the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
explain: «She did a neat pike before she hit the water.» Here pike is a shape your body makes in the air. In a name like «Scafell Pike», pike is part of a mountain's name, which starts with a capital letter, so you cannot swap in another meaning.
fact: Pike has several meanings, so check the context before you choose one.
fact: A pike is a large fish with a long body and sharp teeth. It lives in rivers and lakes and eats other fish.
fact: A pike is also a long wooden pole with a sharp metal point, which soldiers used as a weapon long ago.
fact: In diving and gymnastics, a pike is a position with your legs straight and your body bent at the hips.
fact: Scafell Pike is the highest mountain in England, at 978 metres, and here pike is part of the name.
exercise: «The pike darted through the weeds and snapped up a small fish.» What is a pike here? | A main road where you pay a toll | A pose with straight legs and a bent body | *A large fish with a long body and sharp teeth | A long wooden pole with a sharp point
exercise: «The soldiers stood in a line, holding their pikes high.» What is a pike here? | A road where you pay a toll | A large fish with sharp teeth | *A long wooden pole with a sharp metal point | A bent-over diving position
exercise: «She did a neat pike before she hit the water.» What is a pike here? | A fish that lives in rivers | A long wooden weapon | *A body position with straight legs and a body bent at the hips | A road where you pay a toll
test: «Scafell Pike is the highest mountain in England, at 978 metres.» Why does pike start with a capital letter here? | Because it is a fish | Because it starts a sentence | Because it means a road | *Because it is part of a name
test: Which meaning fits «the pike rose from the lake to catch a fly»? | A diving position with straight legs | A long wooden weapon used by soldiers | *A large fish that eats other fish | A main road where you pay a toll
exam: Priya says pike always means a fish. Which sentence shows she is wrong? | The pike ate a small fish | The pike lives in a cold lake | The pike swam under the bridge | *The soldiers carried pikes into battle
